{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "calibration_diagnostics.v8.schema.json",
  "$comment": "Generated from microcosm.diagnostics.CalibrationDiagnosticsV8. Do not edit it; regenerate it with `uv run --no-sync python tools/generate_calibration_diagnostics_json_schema.py`.",
  "title": "Microcosm calibration diagnostics, schema 8",
  "description": "The calibration_diagnostics.json document, schema 8, that Microcosm builds write beside a calibrated dataset. The pydantic models it is generated from remain the authority. A document that satisfies this schema can still break one of the rules below, which the models enforce and this schema does not express, so validate with microcosm.diagnostics.parse_calibration_diagnostics for the complete contract. Equalities between floating-point values hold within the models' tolerances.\n\nRules the models enforce that this schema does not express:\n- Every number is finite, at any depth, including inside options, targets[].metadata and build. JSON has no NaN or Infinity, but lenient parsers such as Python's json module accept those tokens, and a JSON Schema number cannot exclude the values they produce, so parse submissions with a parser that refuses them.\n- Integer fields hold integers written without a fraction or exponent. JSON Schema counts 2.0 and 1e3 as integers; the strict models refuse them, because Python's json module decodes them as floats.\n- Strings the models type as non-blank contain at least one character that is not whitespace. These are most names, identifiers, labels, sources, entities, reasons, codes and messages, and the keys of uk_diagnostics.target_observation_basis, the zero-weight strata and the consumption-drift maps.\n- Digests match ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ with nothing after them. In the ECMA-262 regular expressions JSON Schema specifies, $ matches only at the end of the string, but validators that evaluate pattern with Python's re module also let $ match before a final newline, so they accept a digest followed by a newline.\n- In each targets[].hierarchy, category.provider_id equals provider.id and the dimensions have unique ids.\n- Each targets[].hierarchy.target.id equals that row's target_name.\n- target_surface.constraint_matrix has n_targets rows and n_records columns, and its nnz is at most rows times columns.\n- target_surface.n_targets equals the number of targets, target_registry.n_specs is at least the number of targets, target_surface.n_records equals n_records, and n_nonzero is at most n_records.\n- A UK registry (target_registry.country \"uk\") requires uk_diagnostics; any other registry requires it to be null or absent. In a UK document, uk_diagnostics.weights.n_records, effective_sample_size and top_1pct_weight_share equal the top-level n_records, effective_sample_size and top_1pct_weight_share. The n_targets, n_scored and n_skipped values in uk_diagnostics.target_pass_rates_by_geography_level sum to target_registry.n_specs, the number of targets and the number of skipped targets.\n- In uk_diagnostics.weights, positive_weight_records plus zero_weight_records equals n_records, effective_sample_size is at most n_records, and ess_fraction equals effective_sample_size divided by n_records. median_positive_weight and max_to_median_positive_weight are null exactly when positive_weight_records is 0; otherwise max_to_median_positive_weight equals max_weight divided by median_positive_weight.\n- In each uk_diagnostics.zero_weight_rows_by_stratum row, positive_weight_rows plus zero_weight_rows equals rows. Across the strata, rows, positive_weight_rows and zero_weight_rows sum to weights.n_records, weights.positive_weight_records and weights.zero_weight_records.\n- uk_diagnostics.target_pass_rates_by_geography_level has exactly one row for each of national, region, country, local_authority and constituency. In each row, n_scored plus n_skipped equals n_targets, n_within_10pct is at most n_scored, and pass_rate equals n_within_10pct divided by n_targets, or is null when n_targets is 0.\n- uk_diagnostics.weakest_families and weakest_areas_by_fit are both present or both absent. In weakest_families, weakest_areas_by_fit.bottom_by_fit and weakest_areas_by_fit.countries, n_within_10pct is at most n_targets and pass_rate equals n_within_10pct divided by n_targets. bottom_by_fit has at most limit rows and at most n_areas_scored rows.\n- A measured uk_diagnostics.rotated_holdout has unique grains and n_folds entries in both fold_losses and folds. Its fold ids cover 0 to n_folds - 1, each fold's holdout_loss equals its fold_losses entry, mean_holdout_loss is the mean of fold_losses and worst_holdout_loss is their maximum. Each fold has n_holdout_targets unique holdout_target_indices.\n- Each uk_diagnostics.consumption_drift value has change equal to final minus design, and its relative_change is null exactly when design is 0.\n\nWhere this schema is stricter than the models' parser:\n- Literal fields hold exactly their declared JSON value. The models compare literals with Python equality, so their parser also accepts 1 for true and true for 1, for example in rotated_holdout.skipped or uk_diagnostics.schema_version. The canonical writer always emits the declared value, which this schema requires.",
